  "body": "**PR 2 of 3.** v5.32.0 shipped the capability data; this consumes it. This is the behaviour change.\n\n## What was broken\n\nA tier set to **High** was sent verbatim to a model whose vocabulary stops at `medium`, and a numeric budget went to models with no budget parameter at all. Reasoning was translated per **provider**, and `ReasoningEncoder.encode` received **no model information whatsoever**, so it fired unconditionally.\n\n## The shape of the fix\n\nThe whole feature is a `ReasoningPolicy -> ReasoningPolicy` transform at the routing boundary. **No provider adapter and no encoder changed.**\n\nThat is what makes the safety property provable rather than argued: an unknown capability returns the **same object**, so the encoder receives literally the input it received before this existed.\n\n| Situation | Sent |\n| --- | --- |\n| Unknown model | **unchanged, by identity** |\n| Policy OFF | unchanged — every encoder already has a disabled path |\n| Known not to reason | no reasoning fields at all |\n| Effort supported | as-is |\n| Effort too high | highest supported `<=` requested |\n| Effort below the model's range | clamped **up** to its lowest — never drop what was asked for |\n| Toggle-only model | thinking on, level discarded |\n| Budget-only model | `clamp(effective_max * ratio, 1024, limit)` |\n| Explicit client budget | clamped to `[1024, limit]` |\n\nRatios — minimal `.10`, low `.20`, medium `.50`, high `.80`, xhigh/max `.95` — are the **published industry values**, in one named constant with the source URL. [OpenRouter documents](https://openrouter.ai/docs/use-cases/reasoning-tokens) `budget_tokens = max(min(max_tokens * ratio, 128000), 1024)`; Vercel's AI Gateway publishes the same table. `1024` is Anthropic's documented floor; `.95` rather than `1.0` because Anthropic requires the budget to stay strictly under `max_tokens`.\n\n**Capability never names a wire field.** It changes the value and whether to send at all; the encoder keeps sole ownership of the shape, so no provider can be handed a field its API does not know. A generic invariant test asserts emitted field paths stay a **subset** of what each encoder already emitted, across 6 capability shapes × 12 policies × 3 limit combos × 8 profiles.\n\n## An architecture correction worth noting\n\nThe first wiring attempt put the models.dev lookups directly in `api/routes.py` and broke `tests/contracts/test_import_boundaries.py` — `api` may not import `providers`. Rather than register an exception, the lookups moved behind `RequestRuntimePort`, implemented in `runtime/provider_manager.py`, which already owns the model cache. The contract test passes **unmodified**, the application layer does no disk IO, and the shape is now the `vision_lookup` precedent byte for byte.\n\nBoth lookups are optional constructor arguments, so every other `ModelRouter` construction (messaging, CLI, handler defaults) gates nothing at all.\n\n## Verification\n\n202 new tests — a matrix over capability × policy × limits × profile.\n\nProbed against the **real 4 MB models.dev index**, not fixtures:\n\n```\ngroq/openai/gpt-oss-120b        tier=Max, model does low/med/high -> effort=high  (+WARNING)\ngroq/openai/gpt-oss-120b        tier=High, supported exactly      -> [SAME OBJECT]\ndeepseek/deepseek-chat          known NOT to reason               -> suppressed\nwafer/whatever                  unknown provider                  -> [SAME OBJECT]\ngroq/...                        policy OFF                        -> [SAME OBJECT]\n\nR0 across 14 uncovered providers x 4 policies: ALL IDENTICAL BY OBJECT IDENTITY\n```\n\nBudget arithmetic on a real budget-only model (`limit.output` 32768):\n\n```\nLOW     max_tokens=32000 -> 6400   (32000 x 0.20)   LOW     max_tokens=2000 -> 1024  (floor)\nMEDIUM  max_tokens=32000 -> 16000  (32000 x 0.50)   MEDIUM  max_tokens=2000 -> 1024  (floor)\nHIGH    max_tokens=32000 -> 25600  (32000 x 0.80)   HIGH    max_tokens=2000 -> 1600\nMAX     max_tokens=32000 -> 30400  (32000 x 0.95)   MAX     max_tokens=2000 -> 1900\n```\n\nMutation-tested — clamp direction, R0 identity, the 1024 floor, a ratio value, the clamp-up path, the output-limit cap and the OFF exemption each failed specific tests by name. Breaking R0 alone failed `test_unknown_capability_leaves_every_request_byte_identical` and `test_the_router_leaves_an_unknown_model_alone` plus 111 others.\n\n```\nfull suite  4664 passed, 14 pre-existing baseline\nruff format / ruff check / ty check   clean repo-wide\n```\n\n## Known and recorded, not hidden\n\n**Deriving an effort from an explicit client budget** uses this project's own compressed budget table (512/512/1024/2048/4096/8192), so any client budget at or above 8192 — most real Claude Code requests — resolves to `max` before clamping. Defensible (a large budget *is* a request for maximum thinking) but coarse. A proportional inverse of the ratio table would preserve gradation and be symmetric with the forward direction; that is the obvious follow-up, deliberately not slipped in here.\n\n**The minimax split-reasoning flag stays unconditional.** It ignores the policy entirely today, including OFF. It is an output-format flag rather than a compute request, and honouring suppression there would mean bypassing the encoder — breaking the invariant above.\n\n**No live upstream call.** Every assertion is against the resolved policy and the emitted body, not a real provider response.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01Y6SPhyApg7trDx44SJqNti\n",
