    "message": "feat(reasoning): send the effort the resolved model actually accepts\n\nA tier set to High was sent verbatim to a model whose vocabulary stops at\nmedium, and a numeric budget went to models that have no budget parameter\nat all. Reasoning was translated per PROVIDER, and the encoder received no\nmodel information whatsoever, so it fired unconditionally.\n\nv5.32.0 shipped the capability data. This consumes it.\n\nThe whole feature is a ReasoningPolicy -> ReasoningPolicy transform applied\nat the routing boundary. No provider adapter and no encoder changed. That\nis what makes the safety property provable rather than argued: an unknown\ncapability returns the *same object*, so the encoder receives literally the\ninput it received before this existed.\n\nRules, in order:\n  unknown model      -> unchanged, by identity (14 providers have no\n                        models.dev row; they cannot regress)\n  policy OFF         -> unchanged; every encoder already has a disabled path\n  known not to reason-> no reasoning fields at all\n  effort supported   -> sent as-is\n  effort unsupported -> clamp to the highest supported value <= requested,\n                        or up to the model's lowest if the request sits\n                        below its whole range. Never drop reasoning the\n                        user asked for; only OFF disables.\n  toggle-only model  -> thinking on, level discarded (the model has none)\n  budget-only model  -> budget = clamp(effective_max * ratio, 1024, limit),\n                        effective_max = min(request max_tokens, limit)\n  explicit budget    -> clamped to [1024, limit] when the limit is known\n\nRatios (minimal .10, low .20, medium .50, high .80, xhigh/max .95) are the\npublished industry values, in one named constant with the source URL:\nOpenRouter documents budget_tokens = max(min(max_tokens*ratio,128000),1024)\nand Vercel's AI Gateway publishes the same table. 1024 is Anthropic's\ndocumented floor; .95 rather than 1.0 because Anthropic requires the budget\nto stay strictly under max_tokens.\n\nCapability never names a wire field. It changes the value and whether to\nsend at all; the encoder keeps sole ownership of the shape, so no provider\ncan be handed a field its API does not know. A generic invariant test\nasserts the emitted field paths stay a subset of what each encoder already\nemitted, across 6 capability shapes x 12 policies x 3 limit combos x 8\nprofiles.\n\nWiring mirrors the existing vision_lookup precedent. An earlier attempt\nwired models.dev directly into api/routes.py and broke the declared package\nboundary (api may not import providers); rather than register an exception,\nthe lookups moved behind RequestRuntimePort and are implemented in\nruntime/provider_manager.py, which already owns the model cache. The\ncontract test passes unmodified. Both lookups are optional constructor\narguments, so every other ModelRouter construction gates nothing.\n\nEvery substitution logs at WARNING. Silently altering what the user asked\nfor is the thing worth avoiding.\n\nVerification:\n- 202 new tests, a matrix over capability x policy x limits x profile.\n- Mutation-tested: breaking the clamp direction, the R0 identity, the 1024\n  floor, a ratio, the clamp-up path, the output-limit cap and the OFF\n  exemption each failed specific tests by name.\n- Probed against the REAL 4 MB models.dev index: Max on a low/medium/high\n  model sends high with a warning; all 14 uncovered providers x 4 policy\n  shapes return the identical object; synthesised budgets match the\n  formula exactly at both large and small max_tokens, including the floor.\n- Full suite 4664 passed, 14 pre-existing baseline failures.\n- ruff format, ruff check, ty check clean repo-wide.\n\nKnown, recorded rather than hidden: deriving an effort from an explicit\nclient budget uses FCC's own compressed budget table, so any budget at or\nabove 8192 resolves to max before clamping. A proportional inverse of the\nratio table would preserve gradation and is the obvious follow-up. The\nminimax split-reasoning flag stays unconditional; it is an output-format\nflag, not a compute request, and honouring suppression there would mean\nbypassing the encoder.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Y6SPhyApg7trDx44SJqNti",
